{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:91b1079c3dd6f0c003bb20f34f3783141cbc55926e6e3001ba18eaba3acc169e", "sample_idx": 24, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "\"skill in solving Sudoku puzzles requires precision and logic, much like navigating the complex weight of climate policy decisions. Just as each number from 1 to 9 must occupy a unique space in rows, columns, and boxes, so too must governments balance competing priorities without duplication or contradiction in their emissions targets.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe temporary nature of many climate initiatives—such as carbon offsets or gas-powered backup systems—can offer short-term relief but fail to address the structural challenges ahead. These stopgaps, while useful, risk becoming crutches rather than catalysts, echoing the puzzle’s rule: relying on repeated patterns undermines the integrity of the whole solution.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nA sudden twist in global energy markets, such as a surge in natural gas prices or a breakthrough in renewable storage, can reshape the entire landscape. Like a misplaced digit in a Sudoku grid, such disruptions demand recalibration—not just of strategy, but of public and political expectations around sustainability and equity.\n\n\\n\\n\n\nThe weight of inaction grows heavier with each missed deadline, and the climate crisis refuses to wait for perfect solutions. Just as a Sudoku grid demands discipline and patience, so too must society embrace long-term thinking over temporary fixes, ensuring every decision, like every number, finds its rightful, irreplaceable place.\"", "logprobs": [{"token": "\"", "logprob": -2.4437606043647975e-05}, {"token": "skill", "logprob": -0.0016066036187112331}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.02808956988155842}, {"token": " solving", "logprob": -0.015920665115118027}, {"token": " Sudoku", "logprob": -1.220703125}, {"token": " puzzles", "logprob": -0.170888289809227}, {"token": " requires", "logprob": -1.3014607429504395},